{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"reasoning","text":"Let me carefully parse the input.\n\n**Transcripts (transcripts.fa):**\n\n1. `TX_CANONICAL exon_joined CDS=101-700`\n   Sequence: `ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA`\n\n2. `TX_ALT exon_joined CDS=101-640`\n   Sequence: `ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAATAG`\n\nLet me count the sequence lengths. Each sequence looks like a repetition of units. Let me parse:\n\n`ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA` — hmm, let me count exactly. I should write code to do this carefully. But first, let me consider the metadata inconsistency:\n\n- CDS=101-700 implies the CDS spans positions 101..700, which would be 600 nucleotides, and the transcript should be at least 700 nt long. But the sequence looks like about 102 nt. This is an internal metadata inconsistency: the CDS range exceeds the sequence length. The task says \"report malformed or internally inconsistent sequence metadata rather than silently repairing it.\" So I need to detect this and report it, not silently repair it.\n\nLet me count the actual sequence lengths. I'll count with code later, but eyeballing:\n\n`ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA` is 23 nt. Sequence is: ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA + CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA × 4 + ... let me write out the canonical version:\n\n\"ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA\"\n\nBreakdown: \"ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" (23) then \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" (19) × 4 = 76, then \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA\"? Hmm, let me do this programmatically later. Roughly 23+76+~22 ≈ 102 nt. So about 102 nt. CDS=101-700 is impossible (600 nt CDS within a 102 nt transcript, and the CDS end exceeds the transcript length). Same for TX_ALT: CDS=101-640 (a 540 nt CDS) in a ~102 nt transcript.\n\nSo both transcripts have internally inconsistent metadata: CDS ranges exceed sequence length. Also \"exon_joined\" — that's a description tag, fine.\n\nHmm, wait — could the CDS metadata be referring to genome coordinates? No reference genome is provided, so we can only treat these as transcript coordinates. Safe interpretation: the CDS range is inconsistent with the sequence length. Report it.\n\n**Primers (primer_candidates.csv):**\n\n- p01: forward `ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCT` (20 nt), reverse `TCATTCAGGTCAGTCAGGTC` (20 nt), expected_transcript TX_CANONICAL, expected product 108 bp.\n- p02: forward `GCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA`, reverse `CTATTCAGGTCAGTCAGGTC`, expected TX_ALT, 104 bp.\n- p03: forward `AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA` (poly-A), reverse `TTTTTTTTTTTTTTTTTTTT` (poly-T), expected TX_CANONICAL, 120 bp.\n\nNow, let me think about what each primer does against the transcripts.\n\nTX_CANONICAL sequence (let me call it S_can). I need the exact sequence. Let me get it exactly from the file:\n\n`ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA`\n\nLet me parse the structure: prefix \"ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\", then \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" repeated, then a tail.\n\nActually, let me define U = \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" (19 nt). Then:\n\nS_can = \"ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" + U + U + U + U + \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA\"?\n\n\"ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" = \"ATGGCT\" + \"GACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" — hmm, actually \"ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" is 23 nt: A-T-G-G-C-T-G-A-C-C-T-G-A-C-T-G-A-C-C-T-G-A-A. Yes, 23.\n\nU = \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" = 19 nt.\n\nIf S_can = 23 + 4×19 + tail. 23+76 = 99. Then the tail: \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA\" is 22 nt → total 121. Hmm, that's too long. Let me recount from the raw text.\n\nRaw: ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA\n\nLet me split by the visible \"CTGAA\" markers. Actually, the pattern \"GACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" appears repeatedly. Let me count the total length in chunks of 10:\n\nATGGCTGACC (10)\nTGACTGACCT (20)\nGAACTGACCT (30)\nGACTGACCTG (40)\nAACTGACCTG (50)\nACTGACCTGA (60)\nACTGACCTGA (70)\nCTGACCTGAA (80)\nCTGACCTGAC (90)\nTGACCTGAAT (100)\nGA (102)\n\nSo S_can is 102 nt. Good.\n\nS_alt: ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGACCTGACTGACCTGAATAG\n\nSame count:\nATGGCTGACC (10)\nTGACTGACCT (20)\nGAACTGACCT (30)\nGACTGACCTG (40)\nAACTGACCTG (50)\nACTGACCTGA (60)\nACTGACCTGA (70)\nCTGACCTGAA (80)\nCTGACCTGAC (90)\nTGACCTGAAT (100)\nAG (102)\n\nSo S_alt is also 102 nt, differing only in the last 2 nt: canonical ends with ...GAATGA, alt ends with ...GAATAG.\n\nInteresting: canonical ends with \"TGA\" stop codon context: \"...GAATGA\" — actually the last triplet if reading frame from position 1: 102 nt = 34 codons. Codon 34 = positions 100-102 = \"TGA\" (stop). For alt, positions 100-102 = \"TAG\" (stop). So both end with stop codons in frame 1. Good design as synthetic test.\n\nNow CDS=101-700 metadata: sequence is only 102 nt. CDS start 101 > ... CDS 101-700 would need ≥700 nt. Inconsistent. Also CDS length 600 nt = 200 codons; but the sequence is 102 nt. So flag both transcripts as metadata inconsistent.\n\nHow should the audit handle this? Instructions: \"Report malformed or internally inconsistent sequence metadata rather than silently repairing it.\" So the report/CSV should flag these inconsistencies. But should primer matching proceed? I think primer matching against the sequence itself can proceed (sequence matching doesn't depend on CDS metadata), but the cds_compatible column depends on CDS metadata. Since CDS metadata is inconsistent (CDS range exceeds sequence length), cds_compatible cannot be reliably determined — we should mark cds_compatible as \"no\" or \"unknown\"/\"inconsistent\" and give a reason. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what's expected. Columns: pair_id, transcripts_matched, amplicon_length, cds_compatible, status, reason.\n\nDesign decisions:\n1. Primer matching: for each primer pair, for each provided transcript, find forward primer exact match (or with some mismatch tolerance?) on the sense strand and reverse primer exact match on the reverse complement (i.e., reverse primer matches the reverse complement of the sense strand). Amplicon = region from start of forward match to end of reverse primer match (on sense strand), length = end_rev - start_fwd + 1 (including primers).\n\nStandard: forward primer binds the sense strand (its sequence appears as-is in the transcript). Reverse primer is given as the sequence that binds the antisense strand, so its reverse complement appears in the sense transcript. Amplicon length = position(reverse-complement match end on sense strand) - position(forward match start) + 1.\n\nLet me check p01 against TX_CANONICAL:\nForward: ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCT (20 nt). S_can starts with \"ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCT...\" — first 20 nt of S_can: positions 1-20 = ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCT? From the count-by-10 above: positions 1-10 = ATGGCTGACC, 11-20 = TGACTGACCT. So positions 1-20 = ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCT. Yes! Forward matches exactly at positions 1-20.\n\nReverse: TCATTCAGGTCAGTCAGGTC. Its reverse complement: reverse of TCATTCAGGTCAGTCAGGTC = CTGGACTGACTGGACTTACT... wait, let me do this carefully.\n\nReverse primer: 5'-TCATTCAGGTCAGTCAGGTC-3' (20 nt).\nReverse complement: complement of the reverse. Reverse: C T G G A C T G A C T G G A C T T A C T → let me write the primer: T C A T T C A G G T C A G T C A G G T C.\nReversed: C T G G A C T G A C T G G A C T T A C T. Hmm wait: reversing \"TCATTCAGGTCAGTCAGGTC\": characters are T,C,A,T,T,C,A,G,G,T,C,A,G,T,C,A,G,G,T,C. Reversed: C,T,G,G,A,C,T,G,A,C,T,G,G,A,C,T,T,A,C,T. So reversed = \"CTGGACTGACTGGACTTACT\"? Let me write: C T G G A C T G A C T G G A C T T A C T — that's 20 chars: CTGGACTGACTGGACTTACT. Hmm wait, positions 13-20 of reversed: original chars 8..1 reversed: G,G,A,C,T,T,A,C,T? I'll do this programmatically later, but conceptually: revcomp(reverse primer) should appear in the sense transcript near the 3' end.\n\nComplement of reversed sequence: complement of C=G, T=A, G=C, G=C, A=T, C=G, T=A, G=C, A=T, C=G, T=A, G=C, G=C, A=T, C=G, T=A, T=A, A=T, C=G, T=A → \"GACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA\". Let me verify: reversed = C T G G A C T G A C T G G A C T T A C T; complement each: G A C C T G A C T G A C C T G A A T G A → \"GACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA\" (20 nt).\n\nNow S_can's tail: last 22 nt (positions 81-102): from count-by-10: 81-90 = CTGACCTGAA, 91-100 = CTGACCTGAC, 101-102 = TG... wait, that gives positions 91-100 = CTGACCTGAC and 101-102 = TG? But earlier I said positions 100-102 = TGA. Let me recount. Chunks:\n1-10: ATGGCTGACC\n11-20: TGACTGACCT\n21-30: GAACTGACCT\n31-40: GACTGACCTG\n41-50: AACTGACCTG\n51-60: ACTGACCTGA\n61-70: ACTGACCTGA\n71-80: CTGACCTGAA\n81-90: CTGACCTGAC\n91-100: TGACCTGAAT\n101-102: GA\n\nSo S_can =\n1-10 ATGGCTGACC\n11-20 TGACTGACCT\n21-30 GAACTGACCT\n31-40 GACTGACCTG\n41-50 AACTGACCTG\n51-60 ACTGACCTGA\n61-70 ACTGACCTGA\n71-80 CTGACCTGAA\n81-90 CTGACCTGAC\n91-100 TGACCTGAAT\n101-102 GA\n\nLast 20 nt = positions 83-102: from 81-90 \"CTGACCTGAC\" → positions 83-90 = \"ACCTGAC\"... let me just concatenate: 91-100 = TGACCTGAAT, 101-102 = GA → tail 91-102 = \"TGACCTGAATGA\". Last 20 = positions 83-102 = positions 83-90 + 91-102. Positions 81-90 = CTGACCTGAC, so 83-90 = \"ACCTGAC\" (8 nt: A C C T G A C... wait that's 7). Positions 83,84,85,86,87,88,89,90 = A,C,C,T,G,A,C? Position 81=C, 82=T, 83=A, 84=C, 85=C, 86=T, 87=G, 88=A, 89=C, 90=T? Hmm wait, 81-90 = \"CTGACCTGAC\": position 81=C, 82=T, 83=G, 84=A, 85=C, 86=C, 87=T, 88=G, 89=A, 90=C. Let me redo: C(81) T(82) G(83) A(84) C(85) C(86) T(87) G(88) A(89) C(90).\n\nThen 91-100 = \"TGACCTGAAT\": T(91) G(92) A(93) C(94) C(95) T(96) G(97) A(98) A(99) T(100). 101=G, 102=A.\n\nLast 20 = 83-102 = G A C C T G A C T G A C C T G A A T G A = \"GACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA\".\n\nAnd revcomp of p01's reverse primer = \"GACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA\". Match! So the reverse primer's revcomp appears exactly at positions 83-102 of TX_CANONICAL.\n\nSo p01 amplicon on TX_CANONICAL: forward at 1-20, reverse complement match at 83-102. Amplicon = positions 1..102, length = 102 bp. Expected product was 108 bp. So amplicon found (102 bp) but differs from expected 108. Status: mismatch with expected length? The CSV asks for amplicon_length — probably the actual computed one — and status/reason fields capture the discrepancy.\n\nAlso check p01 against TX_ALT: forward matches at positions 1-20 (same prefix). Reverse revcomp \"GACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA\" — tail of S_alt: S_alt differs only at positions 101-102 = \"AG\" instead of \"GA\". Last 20 of S_alt = positions 83-102 = \"GACCTGACTGACCTGAATAG\". Revcomp of reverse primer is \"...GAATGA\" vs alt \"...GAATAG\" — mismatch at position 101 (G vs A? let me compare: revcomp = G A C C T G A C T G A C C T G A A T G A; alt tail = G A C C T G A C T G A C C T G A A T A G. Differences at positions 19 (G vs A) and 20 (A vs G). So 2 mismatches — no exact match. Thus p01 matches only TX_CANONICAL (exact matching).\n\np02: forward GCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA (20 nt), reverse CTATTCAGGTCAGTCAGGTC, expected TX_ALT, 104 bp.\n\nSearch forward in S_alt: \"GCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\". Look at S_alt: positions 1-10 ATGGCTGACC — \"GCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" would start at position 4? Positions 4-10 = GCTGACC, 11-20 = TGACTGACCT, 21-23 = GAA. So positions 4-23 = GCTGACC + TGACTGACCT + GAA = \"GCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" (20 nt). Yes! Exact match at 4-23.\n\nAlso, are there other occurrences of the forward in S_alt? The repeat unit might produce multiple matches. Let me see: S_alt contains repeated \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" units. Forward = \"GCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" = \"G\" + \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" = G + U. Occurrences of U are at positions ~21-39? Hmm. Let me find all occurrences of U = CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA in S_can/S_alt.\n\nActually, the sequence is: ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA TGA? Let me verify: prefix \"ATGGCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" is 23 nt = \"ATGG\" + \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\"? ATGG (4) + U (19) = 23. Yes! So S = ATGG + U + U + U + U + U + tail?\n\nATGG + 5×U = 4 + 95 = 99 nt, then tail = last 3 nt: canonical \"TGA\", alt \"TAG\". Verify: 99 + 3 = 102. ✓.\n\nSo S_can = ATGG + U×5 + TGA, S_alt = ATGG + U×5 + TAG, where U = CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA (19 nt).\n\nBeautiful structure. Now forward p02 = \"GCTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\" = G+U. Occurrences: wherever \"G\" is immediately followed by U. In S: positions: ATGG U U U U U tail. Let me index: positions 1-4 = ATGG. U1 = 5-23, U2 = 24-42, U3 = 43-61, U4 = 62-80, U5 = 81-99. Tail 100-102 = TGA/TAG.\n\nHmm wait, earlier I said forward p02 matches at positions 4-23: position 4 = G (from ATGG), positions 5-23 = U1. Yes, G(4)+U1(5-23) = G+U = forward. Other occurrences of G+U: G before U2? Position 23 = last nt of U1 = A. Hmm, U ends with A: U = CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA ends with \"...GAA\". So position 23 = A, not G. Gs inside U: U = C T G A C C T G A C T G A C C T G A A. Gs at positions 3, 8, 12, 17 within U.\n\nOccurrences of G+U: need \"G\" followed by \"CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA\". Position 4 = G followed by U1 ✓. Anywhere else? Within U1, G at position 3 (absolute position 7): followed by \"ACCTGACTGACCTGAACTGA...\" which is not U. The sequence is almost purely periodic with period 19, so the only place where G is followed by U is position 4. Also, could G+U start within a U boundary? U_i ends with A, so no. Thus forward p02 has exactly one exact match in both transcripts (since they share prefix and repeat region): positions 4-23 in both S_can and S_alt.\n\np02 reverse: CTATTCAGGTCAGTCAGGTC. Revcomp: reverse = CTGGACTGACTGGACTTATC... let me compute: primer = C T A T T C A G G T C A G T C A G G T C. Reversed: C T G G A C T G A C T G G A C T T A T C. Complement: G A C C T G A C T G A C C T G A A T A G = \"GACCTGACTGACCTGAATAG\".\n\nTail of S_alt (positions 83-102): positions 81-99 = U5, 100-102 = TAG. Last 20 = 83-102. U5 = positions 81-99 = CTGACCTGACTGACCTGAA. So position 83 = U5[3] =