n that CDS metadata is inconsistent (CDS range exceeds transcript length), so CDS compatibility cannot be established.\n\nAlternatively, we could define cds_compatible as \"amplicon is fully contained within the annotated CDS range\" — with CDS=101-700, amplicon 1-102 is not contained (starts before 101). So \"no\" either way. For TX_ALT CDS=101-640, amplicon 4-102 is not contained within 101-640. So \"no\" either way. Plus flag the inconsistency. Good — both interpretations give \"no\", but the reason should mention the metadata inconsistency.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider: the intended semantics of CDS=101-700 might be that the CDS is from 101 to 700 in *genomic* coordinates, and the transcript is exon_joined... but no genomic coords are provided. Or maybe the metadata is just intentionally broken to test the \"don't silently repair\" instruction. Task explicitly says: \"report malformed or internally inconsistent sequence metadata rather than silently repairing it.\" So the key behavior: detect and report the CDS range vs sequence length inconsistency. The audit must flag this — in report.md prominently, in the reason column of the CSV, and maybe in the status.\n\nNow status values. Let me define the status vocabulary:\n- p01: amplicon found on expected transcript but length 102 ≠ expected 108 → status like \"length_mismatch\". transcripts_matched: TX_CANONICAL (also note no cross-match to TX_ALT).\n- p02: similar → amplicon 99 bp vs expected 104 → \"length_mismatch\".\n- p03: no match → \"no_amplicon\" / \"fail\".\n\nAlso all pairs affected by inconsistent CDS metadata → cds_compatible = no.\n\nShould transcripts_matched include all transcripts matched, even non-expected? Yes: \"audit all primer pairs against all provided transcript isoforms\" — so for each pair, test all transcripts and report which matched. For p01: only TX_CANONICAL. p02: only TX_ALT. p03: none.\n\nNow, should primer matching allow mismatches? The task doesn't specify. Exact matching is the