h ATG and ends with a stop codon: since the range is invalid, skip / note as not evaluable. Actually, if CDS=101-700 were true, the CDS would need 600 nt that don't exist. Report only.\n\nAlso check that the expected_transcript in primer CSV exists in the FASTA: TX_CANONICAL ✓, TX_ALT ✓. If any pair referenced a transcript not in FASTA, we'd flag. None here.\n\nAlso check primer CSV for malformed: all rows have the required columns; sequences are valid DNA (ACGT only) ✓; lengths 20 ✓. p03 primers are homopolymers — we can note as a quality warning (poly-A/T primers are non-specific / unlikely to amplify), but primary result: no match.\n\nAlso check expected_product_bp sanity: positive integer ✓. Also note: for p01/p02 expected product is longer than...? Actually 108 > 102 = transcript length! Expected product 108 bp from a 102 nt transcript is impossible. Similarly 104 > 102 impossible; 120 > 102 impossible. That's another cross-file inconsistency worth reporting: all three expected product sizes exceed the length of the expected transcript. Good catch for the report.\n\nWait, let me actually verify: p02 expected 104 vs TX_ALT length 102 → 104 > 102 ✓ impossible. p01: 108 > 102 ✓. p03: 120 > 102 ✓. So all three expected_product_bp values are impossible given the supplied transcript lengths. Report this.\n\nHmm, this makes me think the intended interpretation might be different — perhaps the CDS=101-700 metadata was meant to indicate these are only partial excerpts of the transcripts... but the instructions are clear: don't silently repair; report inconsistencies. So flag.\n\nNow, what should the status values be? Let me pick clear, machine-readable values:\n- p01: \"length_mismatch\" (amplicon found on expected transcript but size differs from expected). Or should we also fold in \"cds_metadata_inconsistent\"? Keep status focused on the primary outcome; use reason for details. Let me define statuses:\n  - \"ok\" — amplicon matches expected transcript and length and 