TX_ALT; p03 → none (\"none\" or blank). Use \"none\" for clarity. Hmm, or empty string. Use \"none\" — more explicit in CSV. Let me reconsider: the grader might expect an empty string for no match. \"none\" is unambiguous; I'll go with \"none\" and document in report. Actually let me think what's most natural: \"transcripts_matched\" — semicolon-separated list of transcript IDs where the pair forms an amplicon; if empty, \"none\". OK.\n\namplicon_length for p03: \"NA\".\n\nStatus values: \"length_mismatch\" for p01, p02; \"no_amplicon\" for p03. Should the status also reflect metadata issues? Keep the status focused on the primary outcome; put metadata issues in reason + report. Hmm, but let me reconsider p01/p02 status: expected product length doesn't match → \"length_mismatch\" is accurate. Also amplicon not within CDS → that's the cds_compatible=no column. Good.\n\nReason: comprehensive single string, semicolon-separated, CSV-quoted.\n\nLet me also decide about whether to mark cds_compatible for p01/p02 as \"no\" vs \"unknown\": The amplicon (1-102 / 4-102) clearly extends outside the annotated CDS start (101) under any non-repaired reading of the metadata: CDS is stated to start at 101; the amplicon includes positions before 101; hence not CDS-contained → \"no\" is defensible without any repair. Additionally the CDS end exceeds sequence length → metadata inconsistent → cannot be validated. Both point to \"no\". Good, no ambiguity.\n\nAlso, should we include a check for whether primers bind within CDS? Not necessary.\n\nAnother validation: forward primer p01 equals positions 1-20 of both transcripts — but only canonical produces an amplicon because the reverse fails on alt. Good.\n\nAlso let me think about \"exon_joined\" — interpret as the sequence being an exon-concatenated transcript; no intron info provided, so nothing to validate. Mention in report as annotation tag.\n\nAlso, is the CSV header exactly as requested? \"Write output/primer_audit.csv with pair_id,transcripts_matched,amplicon_length,cds_compatible,status,reason\" — yes, use that header exactly, in that order.\n\nDeliverable paths: output/primer_audit.csv, output/analysis.py, output/report.md (relative to workspace). Create workspace/output.\n\nPlan:\n1. Create output/analysis.py implementing the above, generating both primer_audit.csv and report.md in output/.\n2. Run it.\n3. Verify outputs (cat CSV, check report), cross-check computed positions against my manual analysis.\n4. Present summary.\n\nShould I use the dna-rna-sequence-analysis skill? There's an available skill for DNA/RNA sequence analysis. Skill description: \"Analyze DNA and RNA sequences, including molecular weight computation, reverse complement generation, and oligonucleotide properties.\" This task is primer-vs-transcript audit; the skill might provide useful tooling. Let me check the SKILL.md to see if it prescribes an approach/tool. Instructions say skills are available; use is optional. Given the task is self-contained and simple, a pure Python stdlib script suffices. But the skill may have helpers for reverse complement etc. It's worth a quick read to align with expectations — the skill list is provided for potential use, and reading SKILL.md is cheap. Let me read it.\n\nActually, the evaluation isolation rule says only work inside the workspace and use files under ./inputs. The skill files are outside the workspace (codex_home/skills). Reading SKILL.md instructions is part of the skill system, presumably fine. But to be safe about isolation, the skill instructions themselves are not task answers; reading them is normal. Let me quickly check the dna-rna-sequence-analysis SKILL.md to see if it mandates tooling.\n\nHmm, but caveat: the skill directory is under .../T0/codex_home/skills — that's this task's codex home, so it's part of the provided environment, not grader material. OK.\n\nLet me first read the skill file then write the script.\n\nActually, let me do it efficiently: read SKILL.md; then write analysis.py; run; verify.\n\nShould I write out the plan with update_plan? The task is moderately multi-step; a short plan helps. Let me do this: (1) inspect inputs, (2) implement analysis.py, (3) run & verify, (4) finalize outputs. Step 1 is mostly done.\n\nOK let me now write the analysis script. Key correctness details:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Primer audit against supplied transcript isoforms...\"\"\"\n\nimport csv\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import date, datetime, timezone  # hmm, use fixed date? Use current date for report\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nCOMP = str.maketrans(\"ACGTNacgtn\", \"TGCANtgcan\")  # handle lowercase/n? Keep simple: ACGT only; but be robust.\n\ndef revcomp(seq): return seq.translate(COMP)[::-1]\n```\n\nWait, maketrans with mixed case: simpler to uppercase all sequences first. Then COMP = str.maketrans(\"ACGT\", \"TGCA\"). Non-ACGT chars: flag as malformed; leave unchanged in translate? maketrans only maps listed chars; others remain. Good.\n\nFASTA parsing:\n```python\ndef parse_fasta(path):\n    records = []\n    warnings = []\n    current = None\n    with open(path, \"r\", newline=\"\") as fh:\n        for lineno, raw in enumerate(fh, 1):\n            line = raw.rstrip(\"\\n\")\n            if not line.strip():\n                continue\n            if line.startswith(\">\"):\n                if current is not None:\n                    records.append(current)\n                header = line[1:].strip()\n                fields = header.split()\n                rec_id = fields[0] if fields else f\"line{lineno}\"\n                current = {\"id\": rec_id, \"header\": header, \"lineno\": lineno, \"seq_chunks\": []}\n            else:\n                if current is None:\n                    warnings.append(f\"FASTA line {lineno}: sequence data before any header (ignored).\")\n                    continue\n                current[\"seq_chunks\"].append(line.strip())\n    if current is not None:\n        records.append(current)\n    for rec in records:\n        seq = \"\".join(rec[\"seq_chunks\"]).upper()\n        rec[\"seq\"] = seq\n        rec[\"length\"] = len(seq)\n        bad = sorted(set(seq) - set(\"ACGT\"))\n        rec[\"invalid_chars\"] = bad\n        if bad: warnings.append(...)\n        if len(seq) == 0: warnings.append(...)\n        # parse metadata\n        meta = {}\n        m = re.search(r\"CDS=(\\d+)-(\\d+)\", rec[\"header\"])\n        ...\n```\n\nMetadata parsing: capture CDS=(\\d+)-(\\d+); also capture other tags (exon_joined). Validation list per transcript:\n- cds_start >= 1, cds_end <= length, cds_start <= cds_end, (cds_end-cds_start+1) % 3 == 0.\n\nPrimer CSV parsing with csv.DictReader; validate required columns; validate each primer sequence contains only ACGT (uppercase); record length; check expected_product_bp is integer.\n\nMatching:\n\n```python\ndef find_exact(seq, motif):\n    hits = []\n    start = 0\n    while True:\n        i = seq.find(motif, start)\n        if i == -1: break\n        hits.append(i)  # 0-based\n        start = i + 1\n    return hits\n```\n\nBest near-match:\n\n```python\ndef best_near_match(seq, motif):\n    L = len(motif)\n    if len(seq) < L: return None\n    best = None\n    for i in range(len(seq) - L + 1):\n        mm = sum(1 for a, b in zip(seq[i:i+L], motif) if a != b)\n        if best is None or mm < best[0]:\n            best = (mm, i)\n            if mm == 0: break\n    return best  # (mismatches, 0-based pos)\n```\n\nPer pair × transcript:\n- fwd_hits = find_exact(tx, fwd)\n- rev_hits = find_exact(tx, revcomp(rev))\n- amplicons = [(f, r) for f in fwd_hits for r in rev_hits if f + len(fwd) - 1 < r]  # forward fully upstream of reverse-complement match start... standard requirement: 3' ends face each other: forward occupies [f, f+lf-1], revcomp match occupies [r, r+lr-1] representing where the reverse primer binds on the antisense; amplification requires f < r and typically f+lf-1 < r (non-overlapping); if overlapping, no product. Use condition f + len(fwd) - 1 < r (strictly non-overlapping). Also require length >= lf+lr? Non-overlap implies length >= lf + lr. Good.\n- Amplicon length = (r + len(rev) - 1) - f + 1.\n\nPer-pair aggregation across transcripts:\n- matched_transcripts = [tx_id where amplicons]\n- For primary amplicon length reporting: if unique amplicon use its length; if multiple (across or within transcripts) report the one on the expected transcript first; otherwise the smallest? In this dataset each pair has ≤1 amplicon total. Implementation: choose \"primary\" amplicon = the one on expected transcript if any, else the first; collect all for reporting. CSV amplicon_length: the primary's length; if multiple different, join with '|'. Keep simple but robust.\n\ncds_compatible per pair: yes iff primary amplicon exists on some transcript and that transcript's CDS annotation is valid and amplicon ⊆ CDS. Otherwise no. Also note reason if metadata invalid.\n\nPer-pair status logic (ordered):\n1. If expected transcript ID not in FASTA → \"expected_transcript_missing\".\n2. If no amplicon anywhere:\n   - If any primer has zero exact matches on all transcripts → \"no_amplicon\" (reason includes best near-match info).\n3. Amplicons exist:\n   - If expected transcript among matched:\n     - If length == expected_product_bp and CDS metadata consistent and amplicon within CDS → \"ok\"\n     - elif length != expected → \"length_mismatch\"\n     - elif CDS issue → \"cds_incompatible\" (but keep \"ok\" hierarchy simple: since we have length mismatch first, status = length_mismatch for our data)\n   - else → \"off_target\".\n\nReason construction: build a list of findings joined by \"; \".\n\nInclude in reason for each pair:\n- Amplicon coords per matched transcript (1-based) and length.\n- Expected vs observed length.\n- CDS consistency note.\n- Non-matched transcripts: best near-match per primer (mismatch count and position) to document specificity (e.g., 2 mismatches).\n- For p03: homopolymer note.\n\nAlso, global data-quality findings for the report:\n- Each transcript: CDS end exceeds sequence length (700>102, 640>102) → \"internal metadata inconsistency; not repaired\".\n- Expected product sizes vs transcript lengths: 108/104/120 all > 102 → impossible.\n- p03 homopolymer primers.\n\nAlso verify expected_transcript in the primer file is in FASTA: yes for all.\n\nReport.md: generate from script with all tables. Write it cleanly:\n\n# Primer Audit Report\n- Generated by analysis.py, date, workspace.\n## 1. Inputs\n- transcripts.fa: 2 records; primer_candidates.csv: 3 pairs. Table of transcripts with ID, length, header annotation, validation verdict.\n## 2. Method\n- Exact-match audit; amplicon definition; CDS containment check; no repair of metadata.\n## 3. Sequence metadata validation\n- Bullet the inconsistencies.\n## 4. Primer pair results\n- CSV-equivalent table + per-pair details (coordinates, near-matches).\n## 5. Data quality and consistency findings\n## 6. Conclusion\n\nOK let me write the code. Also make the script runnable from anywhere: resolve paths relative to script's parent's parent (script is in output/, inputs is in ../inputs). Use Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / \"inputs\". And write outputs next to the script (output dir = script's dir). This keeps it self-contained.\n\nAlso print a console summary.\n\nPotential subtlety: CSV writing with newline=\"\" and utf-8. Report also utf-8.\n\nLet me double-check the p01 forward on TX_ALT as well: positions 1-20 identical prefix → forward matches TX_ALT too, but no reverse → no amplicon; the near-match note for the reverse primer on TX_ALT (2 mismatches at tail) should be included. My best_near_match will find (2, 82) 0-based → 1-based 83. Good.\n\np02 on TX_CANONICAL: forward exact at 4-23; reverse revcomp best near-match 2 mismatches at position 83. Note both forward and reverse near-matches for any primer without exact match on that transcript;